==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{term|anvahen||lang=gmh|to grab on}}, {{term|an||lang=gmh|on}} + {{term|vahen||lang=gmh|to grab}}, corresponding to {{prefix|an|fangen|lang=de}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈʔanfaŋən/|lang=de}}
* {{hyphenation|an|fan|gen}}
* {{audio|De-at-anfangen.ogg|audio (Austria)}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=7|fängt an|fing an|angefangen}}

# {{context|transitive|or|intransitive|lang=de}} to [[begin]]; to [[start]]; to [[commence]]

====Usage notes====
* Colloquially, in western Germany, the auxiliary {{term|sein|lang=de}} is not seldom used: ''Ich bin angefangen.'' – I have begun. (Compare Dutch {{term|beginnen|lang=nl}} "to begin", which also takes the auxiliary {{term|zijn|lang=nl}}.)

* The transitive use of ''anfangen'' is colloquial, at least with a verbal noun as object: ''Er hat das Rauchen angefangen''. – "He has started smoking." In the standard language, it is preferable to say: ''Er hat '''mit''' dem Rauchen angefangen''. Or else: ''Er hat angefangen '''zu''' rauchen''. With a regular noun, the transitive use is more acceptable: ''Er hat die Arbeit angefangen.'' – "He has started the work." Although the standard still prefers to say: ''Er hat '''mit''' der Arbeit angefangen''.

